# Which Vault accounts reaches, and how it authenticates.
#
# The Vault holds the host's Ed25519 signing key and the Transit key that seals
# every API key, connector credential, MFA secret and WebAuthn credential.
#
# Locally, accounts uses the composed `vault` service's own projection: its
# address over loopback, and a token the platform provisions.
#
# OUTSIDE THE LOCAL ENVIRONMENT that projection is never inherited — a deployed
# product that fell back to an in-memory development Vault is the incident this
# binding exists to prevent. The composition names the cell's Vault here and
# supplies an AppRole credential as a secret:
#
#   VAULT_ADDR            the cell Vault's address. REQUIRED outside local;
#                         empty there is refused by name rather than falling
#                         back to the composed `vault` service.
#   VAULT_AUTH_METHOD     `approle` outside local; `token` is the local shape
#                         and is refused by name anywhere else.
#   VAULT_APPROLE_MOUNT   the auth method's mount path; default `approle`.
#
# and, in the `vault` SECRET group (vault.secret.env), the credential itself:
#
#   VAULT_APPROLE_ROLE_ID    identifies the Vault role
#   VAULT_APPROLE_SECRET_ID  proves the holder may assume it
#
# Nothing in this binding is a file. In-cluster transport security belongs to
# the mesh, so a cell Vault listens without TLS of its own: there is no CA to
# pin and no token to project, and Codefly delivers values and secrets rather
# than mounts. accounts logs in with the credential, holds the Vault token it
# receives in memory only, renews it before its lease runs out, and logs in
# again when renewal fails or Vault refuses it — so no long-lived Vault token,
# and never a root token, is delivered to the pod.
#
# Cleartext http off loopback is admitted by exactly one thing: the
# composition's assertion in the `internal-transport` group that every
# in-cluster hop is carried by a mutually authenticated mesh —
#
#   internal-transport/mesh-protected=true
#
# — and then only for an in-cluster Kubernetes Service address, which is exactly
#
#   <service>.<namespace>.svc            or   <service>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local
#
# and nothing else. Neither half is enough alone: a hostname says nothing about
# whether a mesh wraps the wire, and the assertion covers only what a mesh can
# cover, so an external name, a bare IP, a short name — or an `svc` label buried
# in somebody else's domain, like vault.vault.svc.example.com, which resolves on
# the public internet — stays refused with the assertion in place. The suffix is
# matched against the default cluster domain rather than inferred from whatever
# follows `svc`; a cell with a custom cluster domain uses the unqualified
# three-label form, which resolves in-cluster under any domain.
#
# Loopback admits plaintext in a LOCAL run only. A deployed runtime gets no
# loopback exemption: a cell naming a loopback Vault would be reading its
# secrets from something inside its own pod — the in-memory store this binding
# exists to stop — and would reach it without the composition asserting
# anything.
#
# The Vault policy the role needs, exactly — `encrypt`, `decrypt` and `hmac` are
# operations on their own endpoints, not ACL capabilities, and
# transit/keys/api-keys manages key METADATA, so granting that path leaves every
# encrypt call answering 403 while the signing-key read works:
#
#   path "secret/data/jwt-signing-key" { capabilities = ["read"] }
#   path "transit/encrypt/api-keys"    { capabilities = ["update"] }
#   path "transit/decrypt/api-keys"    { capabilities = ["update"] }
#   path "transit/hmac/api-keys"       { capabilities = ["update"] }
#
# Creating the key is the Vault service's own provisioning, never this role's.
VAULT_ADDR=
VAULT_AUTH_METHOD=token
VAULT_APPROLE_MOUNT=

# The command the cell uses to seed secret/data/jwt-signing-key from its durable
# identity seed, as free text. accounts quotes it verbatim — collapsed to one
# line and bounded to 200 characters — in the refusal it raises when the signing
# key is missing, so the operator reading a crash loop sees the command to run.
#
# This module never names that command itself: a module names nothing above it,
# and the verb belongs to the cell's runbook. Never put a credential here.
#
# REQUIRED outside the local environment: accounts refuses to start without it,
# by name. A diagnostic that exists only when somebody remembered to configure
# it is missing exactly when the incident happens, so the check runs at boot —
# while a deployment can still be fixed — rather than during a crash loop. A
# local run has no cell and no seeding command, so it is optional there and the
# refusal points at module/KEY_ROTATION.md instead.
VAULT_KEY_CUSTODY=
